Flash contains the program we built. 
- machine instructions
- constants                              -----> Instruction Feteched to CPU (CPU fetches it)
- program logic            
- lookup tables, etc

The CPU fetches instructions from Flashes, then executes them in the CPU.

At 16 MHz, Flash may be fast enough to provide the requested data with few/no wait states.

CPU at 168 MHz asks for an instruction to quick and Flash will need a little more time resulting
"wait states" and Flash provides the instruction, and CPU exeucutes it.

So, wait states don't make Flash Faster. They tell the CPU/Flash interface: 
"Don't expect the Flash data immediately; give Flash N CPU cycles to respond."


What happens if latency is too low?

CPU @ 168 MHz
      │
      │ fetch instruction
      ▼
Flash controller
      │
      │ ← insufficient wait states
      ▼
❌ invalid/unavailable instruction data
      │
      ▼
CPU may fault
      │
      ▼
HardFault



The CPU is running faster than Flash can reliably respond to under the current latency 
configuration. If insufficient wait states are configured, the CPU may receive invalid data 
during an instruction fetch, resulting in a fault.